      - `intent` paragraph: provisionBrain's primary output is the
        scaffolded `.aide/brain.aide` (and the seed vault-side files —
        the playbook hub and the vault CLAUDE.md, which stay in scope).
        The MCP entry is now derived from the scaffolded brain.aide
        via parseBrainAide; this module composes the
        InitStep for the MCP write but does not own its byte-level
        construction.
      - `outcomes.desired`: enumerate the planning steps emitted —
        `Brain config (brain.aide)`, `Brain vault`, `Playbook hub`,
        `Vault CLAUDE.md`, `MCP config (brain)`. The first one is new;
        the others are unchanged in shape. Add: the MCP step's
        `prescription.entry` is derived from the scaffolded brain.aide
        bytes via `parseBrainAide` + `interpolateArgs`, not from a
        hardcoded launcher constant. Add: the canonical Obsidian
        template's launcher shape continues to support both Windows
        (`cmd /c npx ...`) and POSIX (`npx ...`) per the existing
        platform branching.
      - `outcomes.undesired`: tripwires for regressions —
        constructing the MCP entry inline without going through the
        scaffolded brain.aide as the source of truth; hardcoding the
        Obsidian launcher shape outside of `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`;
        scaffolding a brain.aide whose `connector` value the package
        later branches on (the field is metadata for the user only);
        replacing `would-create` with `would-overwrite` for the seed
        files (those stay seed-semantic per the existing rule); writing
        a brain.aide whose schema borrows intent-spec frontmatter
        conventions (no `scope`, no `outcomes`, no `intent` — per the
        parent spec's `outcomes.undesired[6]`).
      - Body sections rewritten in decision form. Strategy explains
        why brain.aide is the source of truth and the MCP entry is
        derived; why the template helper is its own subfolder
        (~50 line content, multiple test cases); why the legacy
        `obsidianMcpEntry()` export was removed (consumers go through
        the template + interpolateArgs path now); why brain.aide
        scaffolding is seed-semantic (presence-only check), not
        canonical-byte (the user owns the file the moment it lands).
