### 2. Build the canonical Obsidian template helper

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure`

- [x] Create `src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts`
      and its co-located test file.

      **Function signature:**
      `default function obsidianBrainAideTemplate(rootPath: string): string`

      Returns the complete canonical Obsidian `brain.aide` file content
      as a string, ready to write to disk. The string includes:

      - Frontmatter fence opener `---\n`.
      - YAML body with these fields, in this order, exactly matching
        the parent spec's good example:
        - `connector: obsidian`
        - `rootPath: <rootPath>` (the value passed in)
        - `entryFile: CLAUDE.md`
        - `mcpServerConfig:` with nested `command` and `args`. The
          launcher shape is platform-conditional — Windows uses
          `command: cmd` and `args: ["/c", "npx", "-y", "obsidian-mcp",
          "${rootPath}"]`; POSIX uses `command: npx` and `args:
          ["-y", "obsidian-mcp", "${rootPath}"]`. The `${rootPath}`
          string is a literal placeholder in `args` — interpolation
          is the consumer's job at install/sync time.

          **Important:** the placeholder is the literal three
          characters `$`, `{`, `r`, ..., `}` — the template does NOT
          pre-interpolate. The user sees `${rootPath}` in their
          file alongside the resolved `rootPath` field, exactly as
          the parent spec's good example shows.
        - `tools:` with `read: mcp__brain__read_note` and
          `search: mcp__brain__search_notes`.
      - Frontmatter fence closer `---\n\n`.
      - `## Prose\n\n` heading.
      - The canonical Obsidian prose body — the sample sentences from
        the parent spec's good example, refined into a complete
        agent-facing instruction. Approximate content (the implementor
        writes the final words):
        > Your brain is an Obsidian vault. Use
        > `mcp__brain__read_note` to open files by their
        > vault-relative path. Use `mcp__brain__search_notes` for
        > keyword queries across every note in the vault. The
        > vault's entry file is `CLAUDE.md` at the vault root —
        > read that first; it carries wikilinks (`[[note-name]]`)
        > you follow to deepen context. When a wikilink looks
        > relevant to your task, read it (depth 1) and check that
        > note's links too. Stay in scope; don't follow links into
        > unrelated topics.
        Approximately 5–10 sentences. The exact wording is a doc-
        authoring decision; constraints: name the read tool, name
        the search tool, name the entry file, describe the wikilink
        crawling protocol briefly, name the staying-in-scope rule.

      JSDoc names the load-bearing decisions: this is the SINGLE
      source of the canonical Obsidian brain.aide bytes; the legacy
      `obsidianMcpEntry()` is replaced by parsing this template.

      **Tests** (`obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.test.ts`):

      - 2a. **Output is parseable by `parseBrainAide`** — the most
        load-bearing test. Write the template's output to a temp
        `brain.aide`, call `parseBrainAide(<temp root>)`, assert
        `result.kind === "ok"` and the parsed config matches what
        the template wrote. This enforces the round-trip invariant:
        anything provisionBrain scaffolds, the parser must read.
      - 2b. **`rootPath` is interpolated into the YAML field but NOT
        into `args`** — given `rootPath: "/foo"`, the parsed config
        has `config.rootPath === "/foo"` and `config.mcpServerConfig
        .args` still contains the literal `"${rootPath}"` placeholder.
      - 2c. **Platform branching** — on win32, `command === "cmd"` and
        args[0..1] === `["/c", "npx"]`. On POSIX, `command === "npx"`.
        Use `vi.mock("node:os", ...)` to mock `platform()` per the
        existing test pattern.
      - 2d. **Parsed prose body is non-empty and contains the expected
        sentinel phrases** — assert the prose contains
        `mcp__brain__read_note`, `CLAUDE.md`, `wikilink`. This guards
        against silent template breakage that strips the body.
      - 2e. **`interpolateArgs` round-trip** — given the parsed config,
        `interpolateArgs(config)` returns the args list with
        `${rootPath}` replaced by the literal `rootPath` value. This
        verifies that the template's substitution surface lines up
        exactly with the parser's expectations.

### 3. Rewrite `provisionBrain/index.ts`

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/data-flow`

- [x] Rewrite `src/service/install/provisionBrain/index.ts`. The
      function signature is unchanged: `default async function
      provisionBrain(brainPath: string, mcpConfigPath: string):
      Promise<InitStep[]>`.

      The new pipeline:

      1. **Build the brain.aide planning step.** Compute the host
         path: `join(<projectRoot>, ".aide", "brain.aide")`. The
         caller passes `mcpConfigPath` (which already encodes the
         project root via `join(projectRoot, ".mcp.json")`); derive
         project root by `dirname(mcpConfigPath)`, OR change the
         signature to accept project root directly. **Decision:**
         change the signature to accept `projectRoot` instead of
         `mcpConfigPath`, so the helper derives both paths from one
         clean input. Update `src/service/install/index.ts` (the
         install orchestrator) to pass `projectRoot` and the
         framework-resolved `mcpConfigPath` separately. **(See step
         3a below for the resulting signature.)**

         Check disk for `.aide/brain.aide`. If present →
         `{ name: "Brain config", status: "exists", category:
         "brain", filePath }`. If absent → call
         `obsidianBrainAideTemplate(brainPath)`, return
         `{ name: "Brain config", status: "would-create", category:
         "brain", filePath, content: <template output> }`.

         Seed-semantic idempotency: the brain.aide file is
         user-owned the moment it lands. The helper never returns
         `would-overwrite` for it. This matches how the playbook
         hub and vault CLAUDE.md steps already work.

      2. **Vault scaffolding step** (unchanged in shape) —
         `Brain vault` step. Same logic as before: `exists` if the
         vault directory is non-empty or has `.obsidian/`,
         `would-create` with the directories list otherwise.

      3. **Playbook hub step** (unchanged) — seed-semantic, present
         or not, never `would-overwrite`.

      4. **Vault CLAUDE.md step** (unchanged) — seed-semantic.

      5. **MCP config step — derived from scaffolded brain.aide.**
         This is the load-bearing change. The new pipeline:

         a. After deciding what would be written for brain.aide
            (the `content` from step 1, OR the existing on-disk
            content if step 1 returned `exists`), parse it via
            `parseBrainAide` (or in the `would-create` case, parse
            the in-memory string via a sibling helper that takes
            bytes — design choice in step 3b below).
         b. From the parsed config, compute `expectedEntry =
            { command: config.mcpServerConfig.command,
              args: interpolateArgs(config) }`.
         c. Read the existing `.mcp.json`. Walk the same four
            on-disk states the existing helper does:
            - Cold (no `brain`, no `obsidian` keys) → `would-create`
              with `prescription: { key: "brain", entry:
              expectedEntry }`.
            - Legacy (`obsidian` only) → `would-overwrite` with
              `prescription: { key: "brain", entry: expectedEntry }`.
              The orphan `obsidian` cleanup is a separate follow-up
              step; this step writes the new `brain` key only.
            - Transitional (both `obsidian` and `brain`) →
              `would-overwrite` with the same prescription.
            - Idempotent (`brain` already present, entry equals
              `expectedEntry`) → `exists`.
            - Brain present but entry differs → `would-overwrite`.
            - Malformed JSON → `would-create` with
              `configMalformed: true`.

         d. The MCP step's prescription is ALWAYS derived from the
            scaffolded brain.aide bytes — never constructed inline
            from `obsidianMcpEntry(brainPath)` or any equivalent
            launcher constant. This is the canonical-source-of-truth
            invariant.

      Return all five InitStep items in order: `[brainAideStep,
      vaultStep, playbookHubStep, vaultClaudeMdStep, mcpStep]`.

      Sub-decisions:

      - 3a. **Signature change to take projectRoot.** Update the
        export signature from `(brainPath: string, mcpConfigPath:
        string)` to `(projectRoot: string, brainPath: string,
        mcpConfigPath: string)`. Update the single caller
        (`src/service/install/index.ts`) to pass all three. The
        `brainPath` (vault location) and `projectRoot` (host project)
        are different things; the existing signature conflated them
        because brain.aide didn't exist as a separate target.

      - 3b. **Parse from in-memory bytes.** The MCP step needs to
        derive `expectedEntry` from the brain.aide content the
        scaffolder is about to write OR from what's already on disk.
        For the `would-create` path, the bytes are in memory (from
        `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`). For `exists`, parse from
        disk via `parseBrainAide`. Add a small helper —
        `parseBrainAideFromString(content: string):
        ParseBrainAideResult` — exported from
        `@/service/parseBrainAide` (this is a Plan 2 amendment;
        confirm with the architect before proceeding). Alternative:
        always parse from disk by writing the temp file first.
        **Decision:** add `parseBrainAideFromString` to
        `parseBrainAide` as a small adjunct export — it's the same
        parsing logic, just sourced from a string instead of a file.
        Update Plan 2's tests to cover it. **For this plan, treat
        it as a contract this module consumes; if the architect
        finds it doesn't fit, fall back to writing-then-parsing.**

      Tier 3 inline comments narrate each pipeline step in the
      orchestrator body.

