## Decisions

- **brain.aide is the SINGLE source of truth for the MCP entry; the
  MCP step derives from it.** This is the load-bearing pattern
  change. The legacy `obsidianMcpEntry()` constant returned a
  hardcoded launcher shape; the new pipeline writes a template,
  parses it back, interpolates, and uses the result. Any future
  user-side customization of brain.aide flows through to `.mcp.json`
  automatically because the package no longer has its own
  competing source.
- **`obsidianBrainAideTemplate` lives in its own helper subfolder.**
  ~50 lines of templated content with multiple test cases (parse
  round-trip, platform branching, prose-content invariants). The
  playbook's threshold for splitting a helper is around 50 lines;
  this hits it. Inline keeping it in `index.ts` would also force
  the orchestrator file to carry the entire prose body, which makes
  the orchestrator unreadable.
- **Signature change to take `projectRoot`.** The existing signature
  conflated `brainPath` (vault location) and `mcpConfigPath` (where
  `.mcp.json` lives in the host) — the new pipeline needs the host
  project root explicitly to derive `.aide/brain.aide`'s path.
  Updating one caller is mechanical; the signature shift makes the
  module's contract clearer.
- **`parseBrainAideFromString` is added to `parseBrainAide` as part
  of this plan's contract with Plan 2.** Plan 2 ships
  `parseBrainAide(root: string)` which reads from disk; this plan
  needs to parse the in-memory template bytes before they are
  written. The cleanest fix is a small adjunct export. **Action:
  this plan's step 3b lists this as a contract dependency on Plan 2;
  the architect amends Plan 2's step 2 to include
  `parseBrainAideFromString(content: string):
  ParseBrainAideResult` as a sibling export.** (Marked in the
  open-questions handback at the end.)
- **Cold-install placeholder MCP step has no prescription.** Today's
  placeholder includes `obsidianMcpEntry("")` so the orchestrator
  can detect the empty path and prompt for it. The new world has
  no such fallback construction; brain.aide must exist before an
  MCP entry can be derived. The orchestrator's `/aide:brain config`
  flow scaffolds brain.aide first, then the install service can
  derive the MCP entry. This is a small behavior change that
  preserves the parent spec's invariant: the package never owns
  the MCP entry; the user's brain.aide does.

